 1 big thing: Huang&#x27;s exit triggers an AI earthquake 
 
 By Zachary Basu and Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang. Photo: Soeren Stache/picture alliance via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Huang's ouster from Nvidia has plunged the AI industry into uncharted territory — a corporate earthquake that dwarfs even the chaos unleashed by Sam Altman's firing from OpenAI in 2023.

 Why it matters: Nvidia is the backbone of the global AI economy , with its chips, investments and financial guarantees sustaining an entire ecosystem of companies worth trillions of dollars.

 At the center of that vast financial flywheel has been Huang, who spent 33 years making himself synonymous with Nvidia — and Nvidia synonymous with the AI revolution. "This is no ordinary CEO. This is no ordinary company. This is no ordinary sector. This is no ordinary time," economist Mohamed El-Erian wrote on X , warning of contagion across the AI industry. Zoom in: As recently as two weeks ago, the notion of Nvidia without Jensen was unthinkable.

 Even after his affair with a suspected Chinese spy came to light, President Trump and Nvidia's board stood behind him. Silicon Valley's elite rallied to his defense, and betting markets suggested he would survive. It took revelations of a second affair — this time with an executive who reported directly to Huang — to shatter that consensus and bring his storied reign to a stunning end. Driving the news: Nvidia announced Huang's departure from both his CEO spot and the company's board early this morning, following a late-night board meeting yesterday.

 The board, now chaired by director Jones, has launched a search for a new chief executive, with CFO Kress serving in the interim top spot. "Colette has helped lead NVIDIA through a period of extraordinary growth and has a deep understanding of our business, customers, operations and financial position," Jones said in a statement. Nvidia, in its brief statement, did not offer further comment on the allegations against Huang; the company is set to report earnings on Tuesday. The market reaction has been brutal. Nvidia had already shed more than $1 trillion in market value since the initial scandal erupted, with shares plunging again overnight.

 Famed tech bull Dan Ives said Nvidia was worth 25% less without Huang. The big picture: Huang's downfall comes amid mounting anxiety over the AI industry's future, as new financial, political and regulatory risks threaten one of the largest infrastructure build-outs in history.

 The timing is especially fraught for Anthropic , whose historic IPO — backed by a $10 billion commitment from Nvidia — looms as a referendum on Wall Street's faith in the AI trade. Huang spent years defending the industry's soaring valuations and dismissing warnings of a speculative frenzy. Now his own scandals could help burst the bubble he insisted didn't exist. What to watch: The blast radius could extend far beyond Silicon Valley and Wall Street, threatening to engulf the Trump administration in a scandal over its handling of sensitive technology exports to China.

 Huang forged a close alliance with Trump and Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick, who loosened restrictions on chip sales even as other officials raised concerns about Chinese espionage and smuggling (see below). Top Senate Democrats are already demanding that Lutnick and other senior officials preserve records of their dealings with Nvidia and Huang, laying the groundwork for a sweeping investigation. The bottom line: As veteran China watcher Bill Bishop wrote after Huang acknowledged his first affair: "There is rarely just one lie."

 
 
